---
import ArticleClosingNote from '@/components/articles/ArticleClosingNote.astro';
import ArticleImage from '@/components/ui/ArticleImage.astro';
import { articleHref } from '@/domains/articles/catalog';

import nakatanidouShow from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-nakatanidou-mochi-making-show.webp';
import nakatanidouMochi from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-nakatanidou-yomogi-mochi.webp';
import isuienGarden from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-traditional-japanese-garden-yoshikien.webp';
import todaiji from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-todai-ji-temple.webp';
import deerTodaiji from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-deer-outside-todai-ji.webp';
import deerByPond from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-deer-by-the-pond.webp';
import deerBow from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/nara-the-famous-bow.webp';

const prose = 'article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0';
const mapLink = 'text-[#69746E] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[#4a534f]';
const threeColGrid = 'my-5 grid grid-cols-1 items-start gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 md:my-8 md:gap-4';
const twoColGrid = 'my-5 grid grid-cols-2 items-start gap-3 md:my-8 md:gap-4';
---

<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Watch the Famous Mochi-Pounding Show at Nakatanidou</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Nakatanidou is a small shop in Nara best known for its freshly made yomogi mochi and the famous mochi-pounding show that regularly attracts crowds of visitors. It's one of the city's most popular food stops and an easy place to visit before making your way towards Nara Park.</p>

    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={nakatanidouShow} alt="Staff performing the mochi-pounding show at Nakatanidou in Nara, Japan" caption="The famous mochi-pounding show" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={nakatanidouMochi} alt="Freshly made yomogi mochi at Nakatanidou in Nara, Japan" caption="Freshly made yomogi mochi" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>This was my first stop after arriving in Nara. Since I got there quite early, I asked the staff when the next show would take place rather than relying on a schedule I had found online. One thing I quickly learned is not to arrive exactly at the time you're given. I got there about ten minutes early and ended up being lucky because the show started sooner than expected. I also tried the freshly made mochi afterwards and loved it. Even if you're only spending half a day in Nara, I think it's a fun way to start your visit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Walk Through Yoshikien Garden</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Yoshikien Garden is a traditional Japanese garden just a short walk from Nara Park and Todai-ji Temple. Divided into three distinct sections—a pond garden, a moss garden and a tea ceremony garden—it offers a quieter side of Nara before you reach the busiest attractions.
</p>
    <ArticleImage src={isuienGarden} alt="A traditional Japanese garden with ponds and seasonal landscapes at Yoshikien Garden in Nara, Japan" caption="The peaceful Yoshikien Garden" imgClass="aspect-[7/5] object-cover" />
    <p>I stopped here on my way to Nara Park and was surprised by how peaceful it felt. If you enjoy Japanese gardens, I'd definitely make time for this stop.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Visit Todai-ji Temple</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Todai-ji is one of Japan's most important Buddhist temples and one of Nara's most iconic landmarks. It's best known for the Great Buddha Hall, home to one of the largest bronze Buddha statues in the world.

</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={deerTodaiji} alt="Deer roaming outside Todai-ji Temple in Nara, Japan" caption="Deer outside Todai-ji" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={todaiji} alt="Todai-ji Temple, one of Japan's most important Buddhist temples, in Nara, Japan" caption="Todai-ji Temple" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>This was also where I finally found deer crackers after several stalls I'd passed earlier had already sold out. If you're planning to feed the deer, it's worth checking the stalls around the temple if the first ones you come across are out of stock.

</p>
    
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Feed the Deer in Nara Park</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Nara Park is home to hundreds of free-roaming deer, making it one of Japan's most unique attractions.
</p>
   
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={deerBow} alt="A deer performing the famous bow in Nara Park, Japan" caption="The famous Nara deer bow" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={deerByPond} alt="A deer resting by the pond in Nara Park, Japan" caption="A deer by the pond in Nara Park" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>One thing I learned very quickly was to keep the deer crackers inside my bag until I was ready to use them. If you keep them in your hands, it usually doesn't take long before several deer start making their way towards you. I also found that the deer became much calmer the further I walked away from the busiest areas. If you have the time, I'd recommend wandering a little deeper into the park before feeding them.
</p>
   
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Useful Tips for Visiting Nara</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body">Arrive early to avoid the biggest crowds.</li>
      <li class="prose-body">Wear comfortable shoes, as you'll probably walk more than you expect.</li>
      <li class="prose-body">Keep some cash with you if you plan to buy deer crackers or local snacks.</li>
      <li class="prose-body">Keep the deer crackers inside your bag until you're ready to feed the deer.</li>
      <li class="prose-body">Show your empty palms once you've run out of crackers. The deer usually understand and move on.</li>
      <li class="prose-body">Allow at least half a day to explore Nara at a relaxed pace.</li>

    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Places to Eat Near Nara Park</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you're looking for somewhere to eat or take a break during your visit, these are a few places I'd recommend. You'll find all of them along or close to this route.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/XDWGVrwmRYJDxP7Z6?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Nakatanidou</a> – Fresh yomogi mochi and the famous mochi-pounding show</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/EVLZ376cEyw4Co5e7?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Omitsu</a> – Traditional okonomiyaki</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/U5bfznJjnp9Wwhvt9?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Daibutsu Ichigo</a> – Popular for strawberry desserts and sweet treats</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/aNKgmM1WKoSXS9WW8?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>CONCE Cafe</a> – Coffee and homemade desserts</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/ucFngNJDCyKpEn4T6?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Gyumabushi Miyama</a> – Wagyu beef specialties</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>FAQ</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={`${prose} space-y-6`}>
    <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">1. Is Nara Worth Visiting from Kyoto?</h4>
      <p>Yes, especially if it's your first trip to Japan. Between the free-roaming deer, Todai-ji Temple, traditional gardens and local food, Nara feels completely different from Kyoto and is one of the easiest day trips you can take.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">2. How Long Do You Need in Nara?</h4>
      <p>For the route in this guide, I'd recommend allowing at least half a day. If you plan to visit Todai-ji, spend more time in the gardens or simply explore Nara Park at a slower pace, a full day is well worth it.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">3. How Do You Get to Nara from Kyoto?</h4>
      <p>Getting to Nara is really easy. It's less than an hour from Kyoto by train, making it one of the simplest day trips you can take. Once you arrive, most of the main attractions, including Nara Park and Todai-ji Temple, are within walking distance of each other.</p>
      <p>Throughout my trip to Japan, Google Maps made planning every train journey incredibly easy, and I found it surprisingly accurate for navigating both Kyoto and Nara.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">4. Can You Visit Nara and Fushimi Inari on the Same Day?</h4>
      <p>Yes, and many people combine them into the same day. I'd recommend starting early with Fushimi Inari so you can enjoy it before the biggest crowds arrive, then heading to Nara afterwards.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">5. Where Can You Buy Deer Crackers in Nara?</h4>
      <p>Deer crackers are sold at stalls throughout Nara Park and around Todai-ji Temple. If one stall has sold out, don't worry—keep walking and you'll usually find another one nearby with stock available.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">6. Do the Deer in Nara Bite?</h4>
      <p>Some deer can become a little pushy when they know you have food. During my visit, I never felt uncomfortable, but I'd recommend keeping the crackers inside your bag until you're ready to feed them and showing your empty palms once you've run out.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">7. Do Nara Deer Really Bow?</h4>
      <p>Yes. Many of the deer in Nara have learned to bow before receiving deer crackers, making it one of the city's most memorable experiences. If you're planning to feed them, there's a good chance you'll experience the famous bow for yourself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Before you go">
      <p>If you only have half a day, you'll still have enough time to experience what makes Nara so special. But if I could give you one piece of advice, it would be not to rush. Some of my favourite moments came from simply wandering through the gardens and quieter parts of Nara Park rather than rushing from one attraction to the next.</p>
      <p>Before your trip, don't forget to save the interactive map so you can easily follow this route once you're in Nara. Have an amazing trip, and I hope Nara becomes one of your favourite day trips from Kyoto too.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
